1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
This is Clunk’s answer for one exact dryer: the visible location, the source-backed part that fits, and current seller links—without letting the store decide compatibility.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Appliance troubleshooting crosses a physical boundary. Software can organize evidence; only the person beside the machine can report what is actually there.

3
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:38,000
All eight WebMCP tools are discoverable from page load. Each response returns nextTools, while Clunk’s engine rejects actions that are not valid for the current state.

4
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:56,000
The agent searches the bounded catalog, selects the returned identity, and starts the door-closure guide. The same calls update the page the homeowner sees.

5
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:13,000
Now the browser has to stop. It cannot know whether the dryer is unplugged or whether smoke, heat, or a leak is present. I supply that observation.

6
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Clunk points both of us to the same visible strike. I report the damage; the agent does not invent it.

7
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Only after the report does part lookup become valid. Clunk resolves GE WE01M10007; Shopify only answers where that exact SKU is currently listed.

8
00:01:48,000 --> 00:02:05,000
The same system knows when not to sell. Smoke or a burning smell ends the guide, removes the buying path, and refuses further diagnosis.

9
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Clunk covers 163 source-backed identities and 782 model-problem pairs, works manually without WebMCP, and ships as a credential-free open-source app. One guide, operated together.
